# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Follow the tutorials in order to turn a fresh LAA Spring Boot template into a library catalogue
service. Each lesson has a beginner route, an experienced-developer fast track, copyable code,
verification commands, and a challenge. Both routes produce the same result.

## Before you start

Use Amazon Corretto Java 25 as described in the [Java setup guide](java-setup.html). You also need
Git, Python 3.9 or later, Docker, and access to the
[LAA Spring Boot microservice template](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/laa-spring-boot-microservice-template).

## Learning path

1. [Create and understand the service](java-tutorial-01-create-service.html) - initialise the
   template with PostgreSQL, explore its Gradle modules and packages, and produce a passing build.
2. [Design and generate the books API](java-tutorial-02-design-api.html) - define an OpenAPI
   contract, generate Spring interfaces and models, and use compilation as feedback.
3. [Implement the books service](java-tutorial-03-implement-service.html) - connect the generated
   interface to a thin controller, service, mapper, internal entity, and stable errors.
4. [Persist books and authors](java-tutorial-04-persist-data.html) - replace memory storage with
   PostgreSQL, related JPA entities, repositories, transactions, and Flyway migrations.
5. [Test the service deliberately](java-tutorial-05-test-service.html) - choose focused unit, MVC,
   repository, and PostgreSQL Testcontainers seams without duplicating assertions.
6. [Evolve the API safely](java-tutorial-06-evolve-api.html) - add pagination, filtering, an
   immutable schema migration, and stable conflicts while preserving existing clients.

The tutorials stop before deployment; use the wider guidance for security, observability,
containers, and platform concerns.

## How to use the tutorials

Complete the lessons in order. Every required change is shown on the page with its file path,
code, command, and expected result. The companion example is useful for exploration, but is not a
substitute for a tutorial step and does not require per-lesson branches or tags.

Ruby and Python comparisons are short orientation notes, not claims that their frameworks behave
exactly like Spring or Gradle.

